# Absolute zoom

A zoom button applies a relative factor. A slider needs an absolute value that stays in sync after wheel and touch gestures. This tutorial combines a fixed cropper, a wrapper snippet and the core's absolute-zoom extension.

<Example source={AbsoluteZoomSource} title="AbsoluteZoom" files={{ 'CustomCropper.svelte': AbsoluteZoomFile0, 'CustomWrapper.svelte': AbsoluteZoomFile1, 'Navigation.svelte': AbsoluteZoomFile2, 'Slider.svelte': AbsoluteZoomFile3 }}>
	<AbsoluteZoom />
</Example>

## Compose the fixed frame

`CustomCropper` wraps `FixedCropper`, forwards its typed props, and uses an inert rectangle unless the caller supplies children. It exposes the inner instance through an application-owned bindable `cropper` prop. Its `wrapper` snippet renders the custom toolbar wrapper.

<CodeBlock html={cropperSource.html} code={cropperSource.code} />

## Read zoom with derived state

The core extension `advanced-cropper/extensions/absolute-zoom` provides `getAbsoluteZoom(state, settings)` and `getZoomFactor(state, settings, value)`. Add `advanced-cropper` as a direct dependency when importing extension subpaths in an application using a strict package manager.

The normalized absolute value runs from `0` at the minimum allowed zoom to `1` at the maximum. Restrictions and stencil size affect that range, so a separate local slider value would drift from the actual cropper.

The wrapper reads state and settings with `$derived`, then derives absolute zoom. `getZoomFactor` converts a requested absolute value back to the relative factor accepted by `zoomImage`.

<CodeBlock html={wrapperSource.html} code={wrapperSource.code} />

## Keep the wrapper contract

The wrapper is typed with `CropperWrapperSnippetProps<FixedCropperSettings>`. Forward `class` and `style`, and render `children` even while the image is loading so the stencil can initialize. `CropperFade` hides the contents visually without removing them.

Limit fade transitions to opacity and visibility when that element also controls layout size. Otherwise a late height change can be measured halfway through an animation.

## Connect the controls

The navigation calls `onZoom`. Slider movement uses no transitions so the image follows the pointer; button steps can animate. The source panel includes the keyboard and pointer controls used by the example.

See [layer snippets](/docs/composition/layers) to apply the same toolbar pattern to a movable cropper.
